\section{Value}{


One row per parameter of the fitted distribution -- whichever \code{dist} was
fitted, named as \pkg{EpiNow2} names them -- plus the derived \strong{\code{mean}} and
\strong{\code{sd}} of the delay, which are the numbers most people want and which make
the result directly comparable with \code{\link[=tidy.epidist_fit]{tidy.epidist_fit()}}.

Everything is summarised from the posterior draws, so \code{level} is the interval
you asked for rather than whichever \code{CrIs} the fit happened to use, and
\code{probs} can add any quantile.
}

\section{How \code{mean} and \code{sd} are obtained}{


Not from the family's algebra. \pkg{EpiNow2} can fit five families today and
may add more, and a \code{switch()} in this package would quietly stop reporting
anything the day it does. Instead each draw's parameters are put back into the
fit's own \code{dist_spec} and discretised with \code{\link[EpiNow2:discretise]{EpiNow2::discretise()}}, which
knows the families; the moments are then a summation over the PMF. Nothing
here names a distribution, so a new family works as soon as \code{discretise()}
supports it.

The trade-off is that these are the moments of the \strong{discretised} delay --
the distribution \pkg{EpiNow2} convolves with downstream. Against the closed
forms the mean is exact and the sd runs about 1\% high, that being the variance
a discrete grid adds. \pkg{epidist} reports continuous-distribution moments
via \code{epidist::add_mean_sd()}, so expect a difference of that order when
comparing the two.
}

\section{A name collision worth knowing about}{


\code{summary()} on an \code{estimate_dist} fit has \code{mean} and \code{sd} \strong{columns}, and
those are the posterior mean and sd \strong{of the parameter} on that row -- not of
the delay. The \code{mean} and \code{sd} this method reports are \strong{rows}, and are the
delay distribution's own moments, matching \code{\link[=tidy.epidist_fit]{tidy.epidist_fit()}}. Same words,
different quantities.
